    "lawCode": "Amd §353, Exec L",
    "lawSection": "Executive Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6467\n\nSPONSOR: BROOKS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the executive law, in relation to\ndirecting the division of veterans' services to maintain a discharge\nupgrade advisory board program\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill will require the Division of Veterans' Services to maintain a\nDischarge Advisory Upgrade Board Program to provide written non-binding\nadvisory opinions to veterans of the State of New York who are appealing\ntheir character of discharge from the United States Armed Forces.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. amends Section 353 of the Executive Law to add a new Subdivi-\nsion 24 to require the Division of Veterans' Service to maintain a\nDischarge Advisory Upgrade Board Program\n\nSection 2. is the enacting clause.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nVeterans receiving less-than-honorable discharges are barred from\nreceiving many veterans' benefits on the federal, State and local level.\nAmong them is the important recognition upon their death, when with a\nless-than-honorable discharge, the veteran is not eligible for burial\nwith full military honors.\n\nEach branch of the military maintains a Discharge Review Board and a\nBoard of Corrections of Military Records to which a veteran may appeal\nfor a discharge upgrade. However, the appeal process can take over a\nyear due to the backlog and complexity of the cases.\n\nThe Discharge Review Board Program will review evidence submitted by the\nveteran seeking to appeal their discharge and if they find the veteran's\napplication to be meritorious, they will provide a written opinion advo-\ncating for the Discharge Review Board or Board for Corrections to grant\nthe veteran's appeal. Although this may seem to be an added step in the\nprocess, it will go a long way in helping a veteran to try to achieve a\ngoal of upgrading his or her military discharge.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNon to the state.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the sixtieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n",
